## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to parse mixed log formats, handle multi-line events, and perform cross-file aggregation under log rotation. It assesses proficiency with standard UNIX pipeline tools and robust error handling.

## Key Concepts
- Parsing mixed plain-text and JSON log formats
- Time-window filtering with dynamic cutoff calculation
- Handling multi-line trace blocks and TRACE context markers
- Streaming large log sets efficiently with find and while loops
- Gracefully skipping unreadable files via 2>/dev/null redirects

## Code Example
```bash
# Stream all .log and .log.gz files, skip unreadable
find /logs/trace/ -type f \( -name '*.log' -o -name '*.log.gz' \) 2>/dev/null | \
while read -r f; do 
  case "$f" in 
    *.gz) zcat "$f" 2>/dev/null;; 
    *) cat "$f" 2>/dev/null;; 
  esac
done | awk -v cutoff=$(date -d '2 hours ago' '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M') '
  # Parse JSON lines with ERROR level, extract trace_id
  /^\{/ { 
    if ($0 ~ /"lvl":.*"ERROR"/) { 
      match($0, /"trace_id":"([^"]+)"/, t); 
      if (t[1] && $0 >= cutoff) errcnt[t[1]]++ 
    } 
  }
  # Parse plain-text lines with ERROR level
  /^\d{4}-/ { 
    if ($0 ~ /ERROR/) { 
      match($0, /\[([^\]]+)\]/, t); 
      if (t[1]) errcnt[t[1]]++ 
    } 
  }
  END { for (id in errcnt) print id ": " errcnt[id] }
'
```

## Follow-up Questions
- **Missing timestamps or time zones:** Use explicit timezone handling with TZ environment variable, implement default-fallback logic for lines missing timestamps, or enrich with file modification time as surrogate
- **Large log sets and memory usage:** Replace awk with streaming aggregation using associative arrays flushed periodically, implement parallel processing with xargs -P, or consider sampling strategies for extremely high-volume traces

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests real-world log ingestion across mixed formats, including gzipped archives and unreadable files. It assesses the ability to perform per-hour bucketing and cross-format parsing without external dependencies, while handling missing or malformed fields gracefully.

## Key Concepts

- POSIX shell pipelines
- Transparent gzip handling with zcat
- Format-agnostic parsing (regex for plain text + JSON field extraction)
- Per-hour bucketing and aggregation using awk associative arrays

## Code Example

```bash
find /logs -type f \( -name '*.log' -o -name '*.log.gz' \) 2>/dev/null |
  while read -r f; do
    [ -r "$f" ] || continue
    case "$f" in
      *.gz) zcat "$f" 2>/dev/null ;;
      *) cat "$f" 2>/dev/null ;;
    esac
  done |
  awk '
    /^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2} [0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}/ {
      hour = substr($1,1,13)
      match($0, / (INFO|WARN|ERROR):/, m)
      if (m[1]) counts[hour,m[1]]++
    }
    /^\{/ {
      if (match($0, /"ts":"([^"]+)"/, t) && match($0, /"lvl":"([^"]+)"/, l)) {
        hour = substr(t[1],1,13)
        counts[hour,l[1]]++
      }
    }
    END {
      for (i=0;i<24;i++) {
        h = strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H", systime() - i*3600)
        printf "%s: INFO=%d WARN=%d ERROR=%d\n", h, counts[h,"INFO"]+0, counts[h,"WARN"]+0, counts[h,"ERROR"]+0
      }
    }
  '
```
